WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
Can two white flowers have a purple child?

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.640
Over a century ago, William Bateson, Edith Saunders and Reginald Punnett bred sweet

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.260
peas to test Mendel's rules.

4
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:17.433
They crossed two white-flowered strains.

5
00:00:18.853 --> 00:00:22.393
Each strain bred true, giving only white flowers on its own.

6
00:00:23.673 --> 00:00:25.613
Yet every hybrid had purple flowers.

7
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:29.327
How can two white parents have a purple child?

8
00:00:35.883 --> 00:00:38.223
Here is the simplest model that fits.

9
00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:41.303
Suppose colour is made in two steps.

10
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:45.283
Each step needs a working allele of a different gene.

11
00:00:46.383 --> 00:00:48.803
One white strain is broken at step one.

12
00:00:50.043 --> 00:00:51.903
The other is broken at step two.

13
00:00:53.243 --> 00:00:55.163
Neither can finish the pigment on its own.

14
00:00:56.167 --> 00:01:00.487
The hybrid gets one working copy of each step, one from each parent.

15
00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:04.607
One copy of each is enough, so the hybrid makes colour.

16
00:01:05.947 --> 00:01:08.107
Each parent brings the step the other one lacks.

17
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:11.747
Next they let the hybrids pollinate themselves.

18
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:14.727
Now the deal covers two genes.

19
00:01:15.987 --> 00:01:20.887
The genes are unlinked, so each hybrid makes four kinds of gamete, equally often.

20
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.080
Pair them, and you get sixteen equally likely boxes.

21
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.100
Nothing about the deal has changed.

22
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:33.053
Light up every box with at least one working copy of both genes.

23
00:01:34.373 --> 00:01:35.773
Those boxes glow coloured.

24
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:41.267
Each of the other boxes lacks a working copy for one step, or for both.

25
00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:43.787
Those boxes are white.

26
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.230
Their real flowers came close to this split of coloured to white.

27
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:54.490
Their third report explained it as two genes that both need a dominant allele for

28
00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:00.670
colour. Some coloured plants were red, not purple; that took a further gene, which

29
00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:01.810
our model sets aside.

30
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.600
They found the same in chickens.

31
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.480
Two comb shapes crossed gave a third, and the next generation held four kinds of

32
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.700
comb. One character can depend on two genes at once.

33
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:17.453
Notice what did not change.

34
00:02:18.753 --> 00:02:20.753
These are the ordinary sixteen boxes.

35
00:02:22.193 --> 00:02:23.933
The pathway never touched the deal.

36
00:02:25.193 --> 00:02:28.373
How the genes act only decided which boxes share a label.

37
00:02:29.467 --> 00:02:32.687
Change how the genes act, and the same grid regroups.

38
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:38.207
If a dominant allele at either gene is enough, only the box with none looks

39
00:02:38.207 --> 00:02:39.207
different.

40
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.970
Bateson later used the word epistatic for a gene that hides the effect of another.

41
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:49.363
Look at the second generation of a cross like this one.

42
00:02:50.383 --> 00:02:54.523
There, a ratio in sixteenths with fewer than four classes is a clue.

43
00:02:55.763 --> 00:02:58.623
Two unlinked genes may be acting on one trait.
